A||||12||80's BOY||New Idle problems from my 3.1........this is different please help!!||||5||
Z||000000||80's BOY||04-16-2001||09:31 AM||||okay i had a bad experience with some octane boost. i put in too much and it advanced my timing so much that the headers were glowing half the night. by the time i burned the fuel down to half a tank and filled back up, it was back to running normal again. everything seemed okay, but now it runs ALOT hotter. it used to run about 175-180 and now it's 200-220. it also will not idle correctly when it is hot. it dies at the stop lights. it's an auto, so i have to put it in neutral to keep it running when i stop. the engine only has about 3000 miles on the rebuild. do you think this could just be the engine "breaking in" and i need to adjust the timing? i pulled the plugs and they are bright red like they were overheated, which makes sense. also now it's throwing me code 32's like crazy, when i used to get them only on long trips on very hot days. any help would be great.||||reg||5
Z||000001||mshill||04-16-2001||12:03 PM||||How old is you cat?  I had a bad experience with some octane boost and my catalytic converter.  It was a new cat and it clogged almost immediately after using the stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||04-16-2001||03:45 PM||||sorry, we tossed the cat awhile back and put a muffler in its place. other mods include adj. fuel pressure regulator, Jacob's ignition system, k&n filter, resonated tips, 3.1 stroker kit with hypertech sensors. the factory exhaust system has been rebuilt with only the aftermarket muffler in place for backpressure.||||reg||4
Z||000003||87GTZ34||04-16-2001||11:31 PM||||Did the 'lighted' manifolds cause any vacuum hose meltdown?  A vacuum leak would cause the syptoms that you described and depending where the leak is, may not throw any codes.<BR>BTW I think the boost didn't change your timing but prolonged combustion into the manifold and beyond.  If you have the fiero intake and ecm setup, it doesn't have a knock sensor and won't change timing to meet demand vs knock.  It just modifies timing (delta from set base) to an rpm curve with some other inputs. If you have weak gas or too much advance it will knock and possibly deystroy your engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||04-17-2001||11:32 AM||||i DO NOT want to destroy this engine!! it has been great to me, but i'm a little concerned with the temp. i mean, would a vacuum leak cause some sort of temp malfunction? even so, i will check all vacuum lines. i am also changing plugs, fuel filter, oil and checking all lines for cracked ends. BTW do you recommend bypassing the EGR? seems like a waste of space to me. needs to be plugged or something.||||reg||2
Z||000005||Shaun41178||04-17-2001||11:58 PM||||If you have an auto and it stalls coming to a stop then it is most likely the TCC switch in the transimission.||||reg||1
Z||000006||80's BOY||04-18-2001||12:44 AM||||nope. been there, done that on the first test drive. this is more like a rough idle that dies down very low and then tries to revive itself with an  engine "surge" like the cam's too big or something. the engine HAS been heavily modified, but i have had no problems like this before the octane boost incident.||||reg||12
Z||000007||Phaeton||04-18-2001||02:33 AM||||Hmmm, new motor, ran really hot, now has an idle problem.<BR>If the rings overheated and lost their temper the results would be similar, more gases running past the rings would make for poorer idle the slower the engine turned.  Speed the engine back up and the compression has less chance to leak down and smoothness returns.<BR>Compression checks are quick and easy. ||||reg||12
Z||000008||80's BOY||04-18-2001||02:41 AM||||great idea!! while i have the plugs out, i'll do that. only.....it didn't get THAT hot, just hotter than normal. i mean, what's the norm for a GT about 225-245 anyway?? i have a hypertech fan switch and thermo. i've never been above 200 before on this rebuild, but definitely not hot enough to do damage to the rings...i think...<P>good idea anyway, thanks!!||||reg||14
Z||000009||DJRice||04-18-2001||08:22 AM||||If your manifolds were glowing, then it got that hot...<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tryxalon||04-18-2001||08:41 PM||||80's guy - there is a thing called Synergy works so well on additive gasoline that we used to use it in the 80's to get cheap "high test". <P>If you mix 25% 92 "octane" and 75% 83 "octane" no lead you get a fuel that will test (R+m/2) method as 89 "octane"  (not the 85.25 "octane" that you would expect) <P>The reason is that the proportion of additives is higher in relation to the basic hydrocarbon molecules in active suspension(yada, yada, yada ... check petrochemical engineering references if you REALLY want to know).  <P>This means that your 1/2 tank of "lower octane" did not lower it as low as you thought!!  You would have done better by adding only 1/4 tank of the lower octane.<P>Point remains: You  need to burn the gas out of the car before you can refill and get it back under control. <BR> ||||reg||3
Z||000011||80's BOY||04-19-2001||01:26 PM||||then why did it run better when i added the other gas to it?? eventually, the gas will be out of the system but i liked the theory that the combustion was continuing through into the exhaust. that's why the headers were glowing, right?? and no, the engine didn't get that hot. i have a manual temp guage (autometer) on 2,4,6 and if you recall, the factory temp sensor is on 1,3,5 and they both matched. according to the COOLANT it did not get that hot. now. what event happened when i did this that warrants side effects such a rough idle and the engine running hotter than it has before?? i like the opinions so far, but none of it really makes sense now that i'm on a different tank of fuel. keep trying. thanks. ||||reg||5
Z||000012||88formula||04-19-2001||08:58 PM||||Back to basics. You need to get a vacuum gauge on there and do some tests. Most vacuum gauges have a pamphlet that comes with it to describe the tests you need to perform. Engine vacuum can tell you a lot about engine condition including the condition of your rings, valves, and everything.  <P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
